the list of blue links in search results that are not sponsored. “Ad keyword matching is a long-standing and well-known process that is designed to connect people to relevant ads. A separate process, which has nothing to do with ads, is used to match organic results to a query,” he said. “It’s no secret that Google Search looks beyond the specific words in a query to better understand their meaning ... If you make a spelling mistake, or search for a term that’s not on a page but where the page has a close synonym, or if you aren’t even sure exactly how to search for something, our meaning matching systems help.” It is true that some search queries result in more ads than others, and what ads you get can vary wildly depending on your request, which is the result of the keyword matching Google uses to sell ads. But Google denies pushing users towards the most monetisable subjects.","entities":["ads","Google Search","Is Google Search better than the rest? And is that fair?","organic results","keyword matching","The Age","Google"],"facts":["More from the US v Google trial: vertical search, pre-installs and the case of Firefox/Yahoo -MENTIONS-> ads","European consumer groups band together to fight Meta’s self-serving ad-free sub — branding it ‘unfair’ and ‘illegal’ -MENTIONS-> ads","Twitch’s money guy talks about the revenue split controversy and its monetization long game -MENTIONS-> ads","Expedia -SAID-> ads","Apple defends Google Search deal in court: ‘There wasn’t a valid alternative’ -MENTIONS-> Google Search"]}
